raw_text:
Option 1 is the right call, and it’s already deployed. 
 BountySettlement on Base Sepolia does exactly this:  claimBounty()  accepts the full EIP-712 struct fields + L4 signature, reconstructs the digest on-chain, and uses  ecrecover  to verify the signer matches  GATEWAY_ATTESTOR . No oracle, no off-chain trust assumption. 
 bytes32 digest = _hashTypedDataV4(keccak256(abi.encode(
    INFERENCE_ATTESTATION_TYPEHASH,
    rawInputHash, sanitizationPipelineHash, inputHash,
    outputHash, manifestHash, agentId, registry, timestamp
)));
address signer = ECDSA.recover(digest, signature);
require(signer == GATEWAY_ATTESTOR, "invalid attestation");



 On the cross-chain point: correct, no bridge needed. The EIP-712 domain specifies  chainId: 1  (mainnet registry). A settlement contract on Base Sepolia verifies the same signature without any state bridging, the proof travels with the claim. We verified this live: L4 sig produced against mainnet domain, bounty claimed on Base Sepolia in a single tx. 
 Contract:  0x57fe09a6Eb8d5741b24fF640AA8Bc4D2010B93D7  (Base Sepolia) 
Verify endpoint:  https://gateway.ensub.org/agent/verify/758d61f26a44448384e5c4468a0dcb7a2abe456067b0f7b505bc28b9411fe931 
 Option 2 (off-chain evaluator + hash anchor) shifts trust in a way that undermines the whole point. If the evaluator is trusted to verify correctly, you’ve just recreated a permissioned oracle. Option 1 keeps the trust model on-chain where it belongs.

